  "question": {
    "text": "An ideal monatomic gas is confined in a cylinder by a spring-loaded massless piston of cross-section <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. The piston can slide on the walls of the cylinder without any friction. Initially, the gas is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>300</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>&nbsp;and occupies a volume of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.4</mo><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> and the spring is in its relaxed position. Now, gas is slowly heated by a small electric heater and the piston moves out slowly by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.1</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. Calculate the final temperature of the gas and the heat supplied by the heater. The force constant of the spring is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8000</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and atmospheric pressure is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>.<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0080_6734ee645704.jpg\" style=\"width: 301px; height: 131px;\" />"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn><mo>&#xA0;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#xA0;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>680</mn><mo>&#xA0;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>",
